---
/**
 * S-3 · How it works, and with what — the second half of the Page IA's
 * "what-you-get / how-it-works / what-to-expect" module (Stage C).
 *
 * Components (`SERVICE_WIREFRAME.md` S-3): "Rich Text (deliverables · process ·
 * what to expect) · **Statistic ×n** (optional — reality-capture
 * accuracy/specs)."
 *
 * ── THE PROCESS IS PROSE, AND MUST REMAIN UNDERSTANDABLE WITHOUT MOTION ───
 * The approved HiFi expresses process as three measured diagrams, one of which
 * animates on hover: two scatter fields converging onto shared targets, captioned
 * "înregistrare · toleranță ± 2 mm — treceți cursorul". Two things make that
 * impossible to carry into a generic Service page as-is.
 *
 * First, content: the diagrams describe *laser scanning* — station coverage,
 * drone paths, registration onto targets. They are illustrations of one service,
 * and `Service.process` is a single Portable Text field. There is no per-step
 * model (no step object, no step illustration, no step index) in
 * `CONTENT_MODEL.md` §2 or in `types.ts`, so a three-movement diagrammatic
 * sequence cannot be authored for *any* service, let alone every one.
 *
 * Second, accessibility: the registration diagram's only explanation of what
 * registration *is* is the movement itself, offered on hover and on
 * `:focus-within`, with the meaning carried in a caption that tells the visitor
 * to move a cursor. §14.1 and WCAG 2.2 AA require the same information without
 * motion and without a pointer; MOTION_NOTES.md:54 requires movement never be
 * the only signal. Authored prose satisfies both by construction — it is the
 * same explanation, readable, selectable, translatable, and identical under
 * `prefers-reduced-motion`.
 *
 * So the process renders as the authored Rich Text it is. The diagrams are
 * reported as a content-model gap (an authored, alt-texted step model with
 * per-step media would carry them honestly); they are not reproduced from the
 * prototype, and no motion is introduced that carries meaning.
 *
 * ── EQUIPMENT & SPECS: REAL FIGURES, OR NOTHING ───────────────────────────
 * §10.4 governs this list absolutely — "capture metadata is **real data, never
 * computed**… these are technical claims made to institutional clients
 * evaluating a surveying service". Every value here is an authored string from
 * `Service.equipment` (the Studio field says it in the editor: "Real figures
 * only — nothing here is calculated for you"). Nothing is defaulted, nothing is
 * derived, and an unauthored list renders no block rather than a plausible one.
 * The HiFi's "acuratețe 2 mm · Leica RTC360" appears here only if an owner typed
 * it into that service.
 */
import RichText from '../RichText.astro';
import { localize } from '../../lib/content';
import type { Locale, Service } from '../../lib/content';
import type { ServiceMessages } from '../../lib/i18n/service';

interface Props {
  service: Service;
  locale: Locale;
  copy: ServiceMessages;
}

const { service, locale, copy } = Astro.props;

const process = localize(service.process, locale);
const equipment = localize(service.equipment, locale) ?? [];
---

<div class="sv-process">
  {
    process && (
      <div class="sv-process-body rv" data-rv="meta">
        <RichText blocks={process} />
      </div>
    )
  }

  {
    equipment.length > 0 && (
      <div class="sv-specs rv" data-rv="meta">
        <h3 class="sv-sublabel mono">{copy.process.equipment}</h3>
        {/* No `data-fixture` marker here, deliberately. Elsewhere that attribute
            flags a placeholder standing in for an asset that does not exist
            (Plate.astro); every string below is an authored technical claim, and
            marking it "fixture" would invert the meaning for anyone grepping a
            built page for fabricated content. */}
        <ul class="sv-spec-list">
          {equipment.map((item) => (
            <li class="mono">{item}</li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </div>
    )
  }
</div>
